  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/aulonocranus-dewindti-2/",
      "html": "<p>The genus is monotypic: Aulonocranus dewindti is currently the only species assigned to it. It grows up to 14 cm long and the female is a mouth-brooder.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/aulonocranus-dewindti-2/",
      "html": "<p>Lake Tanganyika. It lives in the lake over sandy areas.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/aulonocranus-dewindti-2/",
      "html": "<p>A somewhat shy open-water fish, but it also likes to look for food on the ground, pecking into the sand in an almost vertical posture. It is best kept in a group; in the wild it swims in schools of sometimes several hundred specimens.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/aulonocranus-dewindti-2/",
      "html": "<p>The sexes are easy to distinguish in sexually mature animals (from approx. 6-8 cm length): females have rounded fins, males pointed fins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/aulonocranus-dewindti-2/",
      "html": "<p>The female is a mouth-brooder. In the wild the males dig pits in the sand that serve as breeding sites.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/aulonocranus-dewindti-2/",
      "html": "<p>Not much has been written about this beautiful fish, presumably because in neutral coloration it looks somewhat inconspicuously silvery and therefore seems unattractive.</p>"
    }
  ],
